# Splunk SPL — Quoting Rules for Dotted Field Names

When a Splunk field name contains a period (e.g. `thousandeyes.test.id`,
`connection.ssid`, `health.overallScore`, `network.jitter`,
`devicePresence.activeDevicesCount`), the correct quoting style depends on the
SPL command class.

**The single-bit rule that explains everything below:** wrap a dotted
field name in `'single quotes'` **only** when it appears inside an
expression evaluator (`eval`, `where`, `fieldformat`, the `eval()` callback
inside `stats`/`timechart`, etc.). Anywhere else — including `stats`
function arguments, `by` clauses, `rename`, `fields`, `table` — write the
dotted name **bare** (no quotes).

Get this wrong and the search either silently treats the dot as string
concatenation, emits a literal column with that name, or returns no rows.

## The two command classes

### Class A. Bare field-name commands → NO QUOTES (never single or double)

`stats` (function args + `by`), `tstats`, `chart`, `timechart`,
`eventstats`, `streamstats`, `table`, `fields`, `sort`, `dedup`, `top`,
`rare`, `xyseries`, `mvexpand`, `rename` (both source and target),
`fillnull`, `lookup` field arguments.

For every command in this list, **never wrap a dotted field name in
single or double quotes**. The bareword form is the only correct form.

```spl
| stats first(thousandeyes.test.name) as teTestName,
        avg(network.latency) as latMs,
        values(thousandeyes.source.agent.name) as agents
        by thousandeyes.test.id

| timechart dc(macAddress) as clients by connection.ssid

| tstats `scd_summariesonly` count from datamodel=Network_Traffic
    where All_Traffic.action="blocked" by All_Traffic.src

| rename thousandeyes.test.id as teTestId
| rename All_Traffic.dvc as device_name
| rename connection.ssid as SSID

| table _time thousandeyes.test.id network.latency network.loss
| sort - thousandeyes.test.name
| dedup connection.ssid
```

The parser tokenizes the dotted bareword as a single field identifier in
these contexts. Single-quoting it (`first('thousandeyes.test.name')`)
makes Splunk look for a literal field named `'thousandeyes.test.name'`
(quotes included) — which never exists — and silently emits null /
returns no rows. Wrapping it in a `by` clause (`by 'foo.bar'`) emits a
literal column whose **value is the string** `foo.bar`, not a group-by on
the field — wrong results, no error.

### Class B. Expression commands → SINGLE QUOTES

`eval`, `where`, `fieldformat`, and the `eval(...)` callback nested
inside `stats`/`timechart` aggregate functions (`sum(eval(...))`,
`count(eval(...))`, `avg(eval(...))`, etc.).

```spl
| eval testId = 'thousandeyes.test.id'
| eval scope  = coalesce('thousandeyes.source.agent.location', "")
              . "|"
              . coalesce('thousandeyes.target.agent.location', "")
| where 'health.overallScore' >= 0
| where isnotnull('network.siteId')
| fieldformat 'devicePresence.activeDevicesCount' = tostring('devicePresence.activeDevicesCount')

# eval() nested inside an aggregate — eval rules apply to the inner expression:
| stats sum(eval(if('network.jitter' > 0, 1, 0))) as jitterEvents
        by thousandeyes.test.id
| stats count(eval('connection.band' == "5.0")) as clients_50 by connection.ssid
```

In `eval` syntax, single quotes mark the bareword as a **field
identifier**. Without them, `eval x = thousandeyes.test.id` is parsed as
the field `thousandeyes` concatenated with the string literal `test`
concatenated with the string literal `id` — silent garbage output.

Note carefully in the last two examples: the **outer** command is
`stats` (Class A → bare `by connection.ssid`), but the `eval()` inside
the aggregate flips us into Class B for that one expression → single-
quote the inner `'connection.band'` / `'network.jitter'`.

## Quick reference matrix

| Command / Context | Treatment of dotted field | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `stats <fn>(field)`, `timechart <fn>(field)`, `tstats <fn>(field)` aggregate args | **bare** | `first(thousandeyes.test.name)` |
| `... by <field>`, `... BY <field>` | **bare** | `by thousandeyes.test.id` |
| `table`, `fields`, `sort`, `dedup`, `top`, `rare`, `xyseries`, `mvexpand` | **bare** | `table _time thousandeyes.test.id` |
| `rename <field> as <newname>` (both sides) | **bare — never quote** | `rename thousandeyes.test.id as teTestId` |
| `eval <new> = <expr>` (anywhere field appears in expr) | **single quotes** | `eval x = 'thousandeyes.test.id'` |
| `where <expr>` | **single quotes** | `where 'network.latency' > 100` |
| `fieldformat <field> = <expr>` | **single quotes** | `fieldformat 'health.overallScore' = ...` |
| `eval(...)` callback nested inside `stats`/`timechart` aggregate | **single quotes** (it's eval syntax) | `count(eval('connection.band'=="5.0"))` |
| `search <field>=value` (base search filter) | **double-quote the field name as a token** | `search "thousandeyes.test.type"="agent-to-agent"` |

The base-search filter form (`search "thousandeyes.test.type"="..."`) is
its own thing — it's how raw events let you filter on a JSON-extracted
dotted field at index time. Always double-quote the field-name side and
double-quote the value if it has spaces or special chars.

## Common mistakes to avoid

- ❌ `| stats first('thousandeyes.test.name') as t` → looks for a field
  literally named `'thousandeyes.test.name'`; emits null. **✓** Correct:
  `first(thousandeyes.test.name)`.
- ❌ `| stats count by 'connection.ssid'` → group key value is the string
  `connection.ssid`, not the field. Returns one row with that literal
  column. **✓** Correct: `by connection.ssid`.
- ❌ `| timechart dc(macAddress) as clients by 'connection.ssid'` → same
  issue, silent garbage chart. **✓** Correct:
  `by connection.ssid`.
- ❌ `| eval x = thousandeyes.test.id` → parses as
  `thousandeyes` (field) `.` (concat) `test` (string) `.` (concat) `id`
  (string). Always returns concatenated values. **✓** Correct:
  `eval x = 'thousandeyes.test.id'`.
- ❌ `| where network.latency > 100` → parses as
  `network.latency` concatenation; comparison never makes sense. **✓**
  Correct: `where 'network.latency' > 100`.
- ❌ `| rename "thousandeyes.test.id" as teTestId` → **incorrect.**
  `rename` never takes quotes on the source field, regardless of whether
  the field has dots. **✓** Correct:
  `rename thousandeyes.test.id as teTestId`.
- ❌ `| rename 'thousandeyes.test.id' as teTestId` → **incorrect**, same
  reason. Single quotes are reserved for `eval` / `where` /
  `fieldformat` expressions; `rename` is not an expression command. **✓**
  Correct: `rename thousandeyes.test.id as teTestId`.

### `rename` — the three forms

| Form | Verdict |
|---|---|
| `\| rename thousandeyes.test.id as teTestId` | **✓ correct** |
| `\| rename "thousandeyes.test.id" as teTestId` | ❌ incorrect |
| `\| rename 'thousandeyes.test.id' as teTestId` | ❌ incorrect |

**Never** wrap the source-field side of a `rename` in single or double
quotes. The same goes for the target-field side — though dotted target
field names are rare. Both sides take a bare field identifier.

## How to verify a file before committing

Run these greps from the repo root. Any hits in production SPL
(`searchbase.conf`, `savedsearches.conf`, dashboard XMLs) are bugs that
must be fixed.

```bash
# 1. Single-quoted dotted field used as an aggregate-function argument
#    (any of stats / timechart / tstats / chart / eventstats / streamstats):
rg -n "(first|last|avg|sum|count|min|max|dc|values|earliest|latest|median|stdev|range|estdc|p\d+|sumsq|var)\('[a-zA-Z_]\w*\.[a-zA-Z_]" \
   packages/splunk-cisco-dashboards/src/main/resources/splunk/default

# 2. Single-quoted dotted field as a `by` clause key:
rg -n "\b(by|BY)\s+'[a-zA-Z_]\w*\.[a-zA-Z_]" \
   packages/splunk-cisco-dashboards/src/main/resources/splunk/default

# 3. Quoted dotted field in `rename` (single OR double quotes):
rg -n "\brename\s+['\"][a-zA-Z_]\w*\.[a-zA-Z_]" \
   packages/splunk-cisco-dashboards/src/main/resources/splunk/default

# 4. Bare dotted field used inside an `eval` (the reverse bug — eval needs quotes).
#    Heuristic: `eval foo = bar.baz.qux` where the rhs is bareword-dotted.
#    Note: This grep can have false positives on string concatenation
#    expressions like `eval url = "https://" . "host.com"`, so eyeball each hit.
rg -n "\beval\s+\w+\s*=\s*[a-zA-Z_]\w*\.[a-zA-Z_]\w*\.[a-zA-Z_]" \
   packages/splunk-cisco-dashboards/src/main/resources/splunk/default

# All four greps should return zero matches in production SPL config.
```

A green run on all four greps + a clean `yarn tsc --noEmit` + a clean
`yarn build` is the "is it ok to commit?" gate for any SPL change.

## When you can't tell which class a command is

Check the canonical Splunk docs for the command. If the command takes a
**list of fields** as positional arguments (no `=`, no expression operator
between identifiers), it's Class A → no quotes. If the command takes
**expressions** with operators (`+`, `-`, `=`, `>`, `==`, `coalesce()`,
`if()`, etc.), it's Class B → single quotes.

You must always explain how this rule was applied and why it was applied.
